> I've been patiently waiting for EV6 to hit the same price point, but I
> don't see it happening.  They are still well outside the 
> hobbyist budget
> and more than what most companies are willing to pay for desktops.  It
> isn't clear to me whether Athlon succeeds due to volume 
> alone, or if AMD
> uses other tricks to cut costs relative to API.

	Let me clear up a few things around the AMD .vs. Alpha in
	cost. I don't think this is opening the kimono too far so
	here goes.

	**It costs more to manufacture due to volume and complexity**

	On an Athlon board .vs. a UP1x00:

	.You don't have 2MB of SRAM cache on the board(have you priced
that?)
	
	.You don't have more than 4-5 layers of etch meaning almost
	 any vendor in Taiwan can make it. Cheap.
	 Consequently, you don't have greater signalling issues.
	 (count the number of pins on EV6 .vs. Athlon) All these
	 add to the cost.
	
	.You don't have higher power requirements. (tho P4 and 
	 later Athlons are catching up there!)
	
	. Athlon boards are made in huge volumes so your component costs
	 are **much** cheaper. 

	You're not going to get an EV6 system for $500 unless someone
	starts buying them at volumes like an x86. Sorry. Believe me,
	I wish we could sell them cheaper! I'd love nothing more than
	to see every Linux developer running on Alpha. I wish I could
	figure out how to get even 30% of them to run on Alpha!
	
									mike
	




