Steve Rogers wrote:
> 
> W Bauske wrote:
> 
> > Sure it does. Athlons should perform better than they do.
> > At least a 500Mhz 21264 Alpha, capable of 1GFLOP can get pretty
> > dang close to that. Athlons are saddled with bad motherboard/
> > memory designs. Same goes for PIII.
> 
> Athlon boards are based on EV6.
> 

The front side bus is but the memory is standard PC.
Take a look at streams performance between a 21264 Alpha and an Athlon.
Athlons are a factor of two or more slower. Even though several Alphas
use PC memory, they still blow away PIII and Athlon in memory bandwidth
because their chipset interleaves the DIMM's.

> > > Lack of software is the problem. What are you talking about?
> > >
> >
> > Let me refresh your memory:
> >
> > "Just FYI here, on the entry level 21264, a single frame render of Lightwave is
> > around 300 seconds, vs. the older "Classic" Athlon 1ghz. also taking about 300
> > seconds for the same scene. "
> 
> Please note that I said Classic Athlon. That has 512k off die cache, running at less than
> full cpu speed. The same test on the latest Thunderbird processors at 900 mhz. comes out
> to around 250-260 seconds.  A full 40-60 seconds faster than the 21264/500.
> 

Exactly what I said. Your vendor is screwing you in terms
of Alpha performance. I have 900 Mhz t-birds and they
are no match for my Alphas on all codes I run. Note that
is ALL codes. In no instance are Athlons faster than my 
21264's.

Off hand, I'd bet there are hand coded asm routines in your
app that use the Athlon's 3D now instructions and no such
code babysitting occurred on Alpha. That's why if you really
want to know how a processor performs, you need to run your own
code on it so you know what's going on.


Wes




