Steve Rogers wrote:
> 
> W Bauske wrote:
> 
> > The front side bus is but the memory is standard PC.
> > Take a look at streams performance between a 21264 Alpha and an Athlon.
> > Athlons are a factor of two or more slower. Even though several Alphas
> > use PC memory, they still blow away PIII and Athlon in memory bandwidth
> > because their chipset interleaves the DIMM's.
> 
> It is pretty much a factor of 2, no more.

Depends on which Alpha you're comparing against.

> I can match speed with a 500mhz. 21264 no problem
> with a 900 mhz. Thunderbird, specifically using Lightwave. 

Thats fine for your use. Many people don't use that application.
Really, you should just go with Athlon because that's where your
app is most cost effective.


> One point that will get the shit flamed out of me for saying, and did at one point,  is that
> Lightwave was poorly optimized for the Alpha platform. (actually not at all). A lot of people
> told me I was full of shit for saying that, even though they were admittedly using VC4 as their
> compiler. Also I noticed the difference in performance between the Intel and Alpha platforms
> were much wider on renders in SoftImage than they were between platforms on Lightwave.
> 

This is still your vendor's problem. I don't think anyone
on the list can fix it. Might get some of the Compaq guys
to talk to your vendor but that's probably about it. I don't
think the guys on this list have budgets to get vendors to
port SW to Alphas. 

> Also, you can interleave the Dimms on the Athlon as well.

I have an Epox EP-8KTA that supposedly does that but it doesn't
seem to work better than the normal 2 way interleave(the D in DIMM).
Maybe just the board but everyone uses the same chipsets. Could
be I set it wrong too. I plan on messing with that some more this
weekend.

Have you actually done that and it made a difference in performance?

> 
> > > Please note that I said Classic Athlon. That has 512k off die cache, running at less than
> > > full cpu speed. The same test on the latest Thunderbird processors at 900 mhz. comes out
> > > to around 250-260 seconds.  A full 40-60 seconds faster than the 21264/500.
> > >
> >
> > Exactly what I said. Your vendor is screwing you in terms
> > of Alpha performance. I have 900 Mhz t-birds and they
> > are no match for my Alphas on all codes I run. Note that
> > is ALL codes. In no instance are Athlons faster than my
> > 21264's.
> 
> You are still missing the point. You seem to think I am Alpha bashing or something which is far
> from accurate. 

You're complaining about price/performance of Alphas on your
apps. I'm pointing out that your app is only one piece of the
market that buys Alphas. Implying we're all ignorant of price
performance issues because you have a problem with your app is
not useful because it is your "problem", not necessarily anyone
else's. I buy Alphas because on my applications, they are more
cost effective than Intel or Athlon, or several other brands,
including SUN/IBM/HP.

> 
> The cost factor alone keeps Alpha from being a viable solution, that and lack of software since
> my only choices are Linux, Tru64 and VMS.
> 

So you're saying you miss having NT on Alphas? I don't understand
your point. Since the Athlon is faster for you, why do you really
care? Seems you've let your loyalty/interest in a HW platform get 
in the way of a business decision.


Wes




