Jeff Sturm wrote:

> Steve Rogers wrote:
[...]
> > It is pretty much a factor of 2, no more. I can match speed with a 500mhz.
> > 21264 no problem with a 900 mhz. Thunderbird, specifically using Lightwave.
>
> Don't think that a doubling in clock speed can compensate for halving
> memory bandwidth.  For a great many apps, memory bandwidth is the single
> most important factor, more than clock speed.

We did some recent testing here, between a 1 GHz Athlon "Thunderbird"
box and a 500 MHz 21264.  The Athlon had 256 KB of full-speed L2 to the
4 MB of 2/5-speed L2 cache on the Alpha; on the other hand the Athlon
had PC100 memory (theoretical bandwidth 800 MB/s) as opposed to our
Alpha's 2656 MB/s theoretical memory bandwidth.

The code we ran *was* faster on the T-bird -- if the data fit into L2
cache.  However, if the data didn't fit into L2 cache, it was faster
on the Alpha.  (The Alpha box also has a couple of GB of RAM, which
I don't think you can arrange on current Athlon motherboards.  It's
also capable of taking a second processor, which you can't do with
Athlons yet.)  

Athlon motherboards with PC2600 (DDR 166) memory should close the 
bandwidth gap, but those won't be available for a while yet.  In the
meantime, we're very happy with our 21264, and we've been able to do
real work on it for quite some time.  :)  

  -- Steve Bonneville




